course: politics
lesson: 16
title: US elections and the president, as reported
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An American presidential election is a bit like a football league where you win matches, not goals. Each state is a match worth a number of "electors", bigger states are worth more, and in almost every state the winner takes all of them. Whoever reaches 270 of the 538 electors becomes president.
explain: That is why the map on election night turns states red or blue: red usually means the Republicans, whose symbol is an elephant, and blue the Democrats, whose symbol is a donkey. Most states vote the same way almost every time, so a few "swing states" get nearly all the adverts and visits.
explain: Recent elections have been hard-fought, and on the hardest moments Americans still use very different words. This lesson says what was reported, gives each side in its own best words, and does not pick one.
explain: A good habit for loud dinners: ask "which decision do you mean?", tell opinions from facts, and check a clip twice if it makes you very angry.
explain: Kids and grown-ups talk about it in their own slang: "y'all" means you all, "fixin' to" means about to (in the South), "delulu" means believing something that is not real, "ate" means did really well, and "rizz" means charm.
fact: The US president is chosen by the Electoral College: each state has a number of electors, most states give all of theirs to the winner there, and 270 of the 538 electors are needed to win.
fact: Some say the Electoral College makes candidates care about small states; others say it is unfair because a vote can count for more in one state than another.
fact: Swing states could be won by either party, so both sides pour adverts and visits into them; recent ones have included Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, Georgia and Arizona.
fact: In 2016, it was reported, Hillary Clinton got about three million more votes nationwide than Donald Trump but lost, because he won more electors; this also happened in 2000.
fact: News reports said Joe Biden won the 2020 election with 306 electors to Donald Trump's 232; Mr Trump and his supporters said there had been cheating.
fact: It was reported that recounts, audits (careful checks) and dozens of court cases did not find fraud, meaning cheating, big enough to change the 2020 result, and Congress certified it, which means officially confirmed it; some people say they still have doubts and want stricter rules such as more ID checks.
fact: On 6 January 2021, news reports said, while Congress met to confirm the result, a crowd of Donald Trump's supporters broke into the Capitol; people were hurt, and Congress finished confirming Joe Biden's win that night.
fact: Some call 6 January a dangerous attack on democracy; others say most people there were protesters who felt unheard; it was reported that in January 2025 President Trump pardoned most of those charged, which means he cancelled their punishment.
fact: News reports said Donald Trump won the 2024 election against Kamala Harris, winning the main swing states and the most votes nationwide; his second term began in January 2025.
fact: Mr Trump, president before from 2017 to 2021, is one of only two presidents to serve two terms that were not one after the other.
fact: A new president's term starts on Inauguration Day, 20 January, with an oath to "preserve, protect and defend" the Constitution; in 2025 it was reported the ceremony was moved indoors because of the cold.
fact: The two big parties are the Democrats, who often favour more government help and rules, and the Republicans, who often favour lower taxes and smaller government; other parties almost never win the big races.
fact: Words like "saving the country" and "wrecking it" are opinions; a fact can be checked and shown true or false.
fact: Videos can be cut, sped up or made by computers; check whether news sites from different sides reported a clip, and look for the full video.
exercise: How many electors does a candidate need to become US president? | 270 | 538 | 100 | 50
exercise: How do most states give their electors? | All to the candidate who wins that state | Split exactly by the votes | All to the biggest party nationwide | Chosen by lottery
exercise: What is a swing state? | A state that could be won by either party | A state with a playground law | A state that always votes the same | A state with no electors
exercise: Why do families in a swing state like Pennsylvania see so many election adverts? | Either side could win there, so campaigns spend there | It has the most people of any state in the whole country | Adverts are only allowed there | Families there ask for them
exercise: What happened in 2016, as reported? | Hillary Clinton got about three million more votes but Donald Trump won more electors | Donald Trump got more votes and more electors | Nobody won the first time, so the whole country had to vote again a month later | The election was cancelled
exercise: What is the popular vote? | The total number of votes from all people across the country | The votes of famous people, like singers and film stars, added up | The votes of the electors only, counted in Washington in December | The votes in one state, usually the biggest one, which decides it all
exercise: Who won the 2020 election, according to news reports and the official count? | Joe Biden | Donald Trump | Hillary Clinton | Kamala Harris
exercise: What is a recount? | Counting the votes again to check them | A second election day | A speech after winning | A poll before voting
exercise: What does certify mean? | Officially confirm the result | Cancel the result | Guess the result | Announce the candidates
exercise: What is the Capitol? | The building where the US Congress meets | The president's home | A city in Texas, where presidents are chosen | The Supreme Court
exercise: What is a pardon? | When a president cancels someone's punishment for a crime | A new law about voting that the president signs on his first day | A type of election | A court trial
exercise: Who won the 2024 US election, according to news reports? | Donald Trump | Kamala Harris | Joe Biden, for a second term | Hillary Clinton
exercise: What makes Donald Trump's two terms unusual? | They were not one after the other | They were both four months long | He served three terms | He was never inaugurated
exercise: On what date does a new US president's term begin? | 20 January | 4 July | 1 January | 6 January
exercise: What does the president promise in the oath of office? | To preserve, protect and defend the Constitution | To win the next election | To lower all of the prices in the shops within a year | To agree with Congress
exercise: Which animals stand for the two big US parties? | The donkey for Democrats and the elephant for Republicans | The eagle for both | The bear for Democrats and the lion for Republicans | The elephant for Democrats and the donkey for Republicans
exercise: Which is an opinion, not a fact? | The president is "wrecking the country" | The president took office on 20 January that year | 270 electors are needed to win the presidency | The Capitol building is in Washington, D.C.
test: «y'all, why they counting states not people, that's delulu» (delulu = believing something not real). What is the real reason? | The president is chosen by state electors, not by adding up all votes | Counting people is against the law in America, so only states can be counted | People do not vote in America | The map is just for fun
test: Ruby: "That's like losing a game you scored more in!" What is a fair reply about 2016? | The rules counted states, and both sides knew them before the game | Clinton actually got fewer votes, so she lost fair and square on both counts | Trump got fewer electors | Votes were never counted
test: Grandad says 2020 was "stolen"; the teacher says it was checked many times. What did the reported checks find? | Recounts, audits and court cases did not find fraud big enough to change the result | Courts found the result was wrong and swapped it | No checks were ever done | Congress refused to confirm it, so the old president stayed on for another four years
test: In class a boy calls 6 January "a protest" and a girl calls it "a riot". What is the best way to think about it? | Ask what each word claims, and look at what was reported | One word is banned, so ignore it | Both words mean exactly the same thing, so it does not matter which one you use | Only the girl may speak
test: The morning after the 2024 election, Ava texts a crying face and Leo a party popper. What is true? | Friends can feel opposite things about the same news and still be friends | One of them must stop being friends, because you cannot share a class with the other side | Leo did not know who won | Ava voted in the election
test: «Harris lost?? but Trump was already prez before, so that's not allowed fr» What is wrong? | A president may serve two terms even with a gap between them | Nobody can ever be president twice, so the result must have been a mistake | He served 2021 to 2025 | He was never president before
test: Uncle Ray from Alabama: "I'm fixin' to switch this off, he's wreckin' the country!" (fixin' to = about to). What could you ask him? | Which decision do you mean? | Who is your favourite news reader? | Why is the TV so loud tonight? | What colour is your cap today?
test: Dylan sees a donkey sticker on a car and an elephant on his uncle's cap. What do they show? | Which of the two big parties each one supports | Their favourite zoo animals | Which state they live in, since each state has its own animal | That they work on a farm
test: «that video of the prez saying rude stuff ate, 1M likes» (ate = did really well). What does his sister say to do first? | Check where it came from and look for the full video | Like it because a million people did | Share it to see who agrees with you before you decide | Believe it because it is on TikTok
test: What would happen if a candidate won only the biggest five states? | They might still fall short of 270 electors | They would win automatically | The other states would not vote | The election would be repeated
test: On TV a man puts his hand on a Bible and repeats words after a judge. What is happening? | A new president is taking the oath of office on Inauguration Day | A court case is starting, and the man is promising to tell the whole truth | A party is choosing a leader | Votes are being counted
exam: Identify the number of electors whose votes are required for the election of the President of the United States. | Two hundred and seventy | Five hundred and thirty-eight | Fifty, one for each state | One hundred, two per state
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the outcome of the presidential election of 2016, as reported? | One candidate received approximately three million more popular votes, whereas the other secured a majority of electors | The candidate who received the most popular votes also secured the most electors, as had occurred in every previous election | No candidate secured any electors | The election was decided by a referendum
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the presidential election of 2020, as reported. | Recounts, audits and judicial proceedings did not identify fraud sufficient to alter the result, which Congress certified | Judicial proceedings reversed the result in favour of the candidate who had lost, after which Congress certified the amended result | No recount or audit was undertaken in any state | Congress declined to certify any result
exam: Determine the date upon which the term of office of a newly elected President of the United States commences. | The twentieth day of January | The fourth day of July | The first day of January | The sixth day of January
